endpoint_correspondence:{
E001:{mode:"equivalent",explanation:"Fourier identification uses the normalized measure dx/(2π) on the same 2π torus. dh/dn are exactly mean-zero homogeneous H^s membership/norm; contH in the inhomogeneous norm has the identical topology by the two-sided weight comparisons on |k|≥1. The sign=-1 coefficient equation is U_t=UU_x+U_xxx, since (ik)^3=-ik³; twisted (-1) is its norm-preserving e^(+itk³) source twist. bit retains uniform Galerkin bounds and uniform limiting-flow bounds with constants chosen before every datum in the prescribed norm bounds, Galerkin subsequences strongly in each finite L^p H^σ and weak-star in L∞ dot H^s, existence/uniqueness/continuity, conservation, H^s Lipschitz dependence, the s=0 smooth-approximation flow and the full theta∈(-1,0] exponential-in-time dependence. The strong L^p condition is convergence of the integral of the pth power of the norm; weak-star is testing the Fourier dual pairing against every Bochner L¹ dot H^(-s) path. These characterize precisely those convergence modes in the separable weighted ℓ² model, in both directions. A common bound can be enlarged to cover Galerkin and limiting solutions, so separate existential bound variables do not change uniformity. Curves are identified only on the declared horizon. All appearances of exact impose the solution relation rather than assuming it. Reflection/sign transfer to the target is proved in T001, not built into this premise."},
E002:{mode:"equivalent",explanation:"cint is the Fourier form of D015 with both projections and the inverse derivative in the third input; constant paths implement the time-independent clause. srcScale=√(2π) multiplies every normalized H^r or L² norm, including both summands of X, yielding precisely Li-Wu's unnormalized norms. cubicBounds at this scale quantifies alpha∈[0,1], every 0≤a≤b with b-a≤1, and every admissible triple, with the negative-norm factor allowed in each Fin 3 slot and the product over the other two. The first constant is chosen before the interval and paths; the constant-input constant may depend on s. aeH and weakD express the given L∞ regularity and weak time derivative through their absolutely continuous coefficient representatives; changing a representative on a null set does not change any integral or essential norm. Weighted ℓ² separability identifies coordinate measurability plus membership with Bochner measurability. Conversely these norm/weak-derivative data give exactly such representatives. H 0 on the output records that the asserted L² bound is a bound on an actual L² object. Normalization is performed in T006, not silently absorbed by changing this premise."},
E003:{mode:"equivalent",explanation:"Spatial multiplication is convolution of normalized coefficients, with output H 0 membership and the L² estimate both asserted. productBounds retains every sigma∈[0,1] and every fixed epsilon>0; sourceAux is 1/2+epsilon at zero, 1/2-sigma below the midpoint, epsilon at the midpoint, and zero above it. Each norm is multiplied by √(2π), exactly the source normalization. The constant may depend on sigma and the fixed epsilon but is independent of the input functions. This is the same product inequality in both Fourier and physical models, without a mean-zero restriction on either input."},
E004:{mode:"equivalent",explanation:"stage is one half of the coefficient integral of B_s(f,g) from n tau to t. stageBounds preserves all 0<sigma≤1, every beta in the closed interval [beta0,1+sigma], n≥0, tau>0 and the entire step, on time-independent mean-zero H^sigma inputs. beta0 has exactly the three source formulas. At sigma=1/2 the phrase 'any fixed sufficiently small epsilon>0' is encoded as existence of epsilon0>0 followed by every 0<epsilon<epsilon0; its constant may depend on that fixed epsilon and beta, never on the state, n, tau or support. Away from the midpoint beta0 is independent of epsilon and is encoded with epsilon=0 only as an unused argument. The time exponent is exactly (1+sigma-beta)/(1+sigma-beta0) and all norms carry √(2π). The Fourier integral and physical stage agree coefficientwise; the estimate asserts H^beta membership, so it is also the bounded extension described by the source."},
E005:{mode:"equivalent",explanation:"schemeIdentity is the exact source algebraic identity in the common Fourier representation: q=S_(n tau) f, and air(-n tau-tau)(step tau q) is the twisted physical update. quad and cubic expand D008-D009 exactly, including S_(s-tau) in the final endpoint product and the outer inverse second derivative. rem uses zero output at k=0, excludes a+b=0, and has exactly -tau/(18 i k), exp(-i n tau Phi) and eta_tau with alpha=3ab(a+b), beta=3kc(a+b). Summing over a,b with c=k-a-b is a bijective reindexing of a+b+c=k, including all zero input modes, whose phases cancel. The right side is f+Stage+integral B(f,Stage)-R2. Smooth mean-zero inputs, all tau>0 and all natural n are retained, with no support restriction."},
P:{mode:"equivalent",explanation:"Together with H001-H003 the parameters are exactly T>0, 0<gamma≤1 and R>0. rate chooses C>0 and tau0>0 before every real mean-zero H^gamma datum q of norm≤R and before L, so the constants depend only on T,gamma,R. It includes existence and uniqueness, on [0,T], of the continuous H^gamma distributional solution obtained from smooth Fourier-cutoff solutions in uniform L²; uniqueness is in precisely that solution class. For every such solution and every natural L>0 with tau=T/L≤tau0, gridRate asserts H^0 membership of each numerical iterate and the norm error at every n≤L, including n=0. This is equivalent to the displayed maximum over the nonempty finite grid. iter is the explicitly expanded fixed Li-Wu step, with no spatial cutoff and no numerical H^gamma hypothesis. hn 0 is exactly the normalized physical L² norm by the specified Fourier-completion convention; in the other direction each physical datum/solution and scheme has precisely these coefficient sequences and equality/norm bounds. Equation identification uses the coefficient integral formulation of the distributional equation; continuity and L² products justify testing individual modes and recovering all smooth periodic tests. The smooth-approximation convention is witnessed and never made an extra hypothesis on the datum. T012 performs the fixed-timestep limiting argument, preserving the constants and all quantifiers."}
